    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "My thesis explores the intricate relationship between investor overconfidence and their sensitivity toward external advice in the stock market. By conducting an experiment, I investigate how different levels of overconfidence affect investors’ propensity to engage in trading and their likelihood to follow trading recommendations, specifically distin- guishing between active and passive trading advice. My study finds that overconfident investors are more likely to engage in trading when exposed to active advice, indicative of their sensitivity to signals that align with their predisposition towards higher trading activity. Conversely, these investors show a reduced sensitivity to passive advice, often increasing their trading activity contrary to the advice’s conservative stance. Further- more, overconfident investors are not only more active but also tend to trade against the advice given. My findings highlight the need for financial education addressing the contrarian attitude of overconfident investors. As such, my study contributes to under- standing cognitive biases in financial decision-making and underscores the importance of aligning investor advice with psychological profiles to improve investment outcomes and the effectiveness of financial education.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
